  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 PM EDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nEnergetic northwest upper flow across the Great Lakes through \nThursday, as several shortwaves pinwheel south around deeper \nupper low over Hudson Bay. The upper flow flattens out by late \nweek into the weekend as the southern ridge expands eastward and\nadditional shortwave energy pushes into the Pacific Northwest. \nA bigger pattern shift begins to take shape next week as deeper \ntroughing develops over the intermountain west with resultant \namplified induced upper ridging developing over the eastern \nCONUS. This would result in a trend toward above normal \ntemperatures by this weekend into next week. \n\nIsolated severe storms possible into this evening as a secondary \ntrough axis drops south through northern Michigan. Still some \nquestion marks about how much instability can be realized today.\nMid level lapse rates also rather poor which may limit updraft \nstrength. But certainly enough deep layer shear with deeper \nmoisture to keep isolated severe possibilities and locally heavy\nrain. An additional pocket of cool air drops south overnight \nwith plenty of low level moisture lingering across the area. \nThis will keep areas of drizzle/light rain and patchy fog across\nthe area. Thursday will have a feel of fall, with lots of \nclouds, cool temperatures, and breezy conditions. Given the cool\nair aloft and steeper mid level lapse rates, a few showers will\nalso remain in the forecast. \n\nMany questions in the forecast for the weekend into early next week, \nas warm air advection begins to increase across the area along with \nthe arrival of additional shortwave energy.  This will likely result \nin at least chances of showers/storms Saturday through Monday. \nAbove normal temperatures make a return for this weekend, which \nshould extend into next week given the developing ridge across the \neastern CONUS.",
